    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1095">

                <pb facs="1637" n="1617" sameAs="frus:frus1950v07/pg1617"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.95/12–2850</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State for
                        European Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Perkins</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1950-12-28">December 28, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Ambassador Wrong brought in the attached memorandum this afternoon. He said it
                    had been sent down from Ottawa and was an attempt on their part to get the
                    situation in perspective. I think you may be interested in glancing through
                    it.</p>
                <p>I told him that the difficulty I saw in their paper was that they wanted to
                    maintain the principles of the UN but at the same time they wanted to be very
                    slow about calling Communist China aggressive. I wondered if these two were
                    compatible. Hume said he personally recognized this and felt if the Chinese came
                    south of the 38th parallel there would be very little choice anyone would have
                    and that they would have to be called aggressors.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
